INT. AANYA'S BEDROOM - DAY

Aanya is an eight-year-old autistic girl. She sits on a bright mustard yellow rug.

She wears a mustard yellow jumper. Her sleeves are pulled over her hands. Grey leggings cover her legs. Noise-reducing headphones rest around her neck.

Aanya organizes her colored pencils. She lines them up by shade. Light yellow. Dark yellow. Light blue. Dark blue. The alignment is perfect.

She reaches into her pocket. She pulls out her totem. It is a smooth grey pebble. She turns it in her hand.

Her mother, PRIYA (30s), stands at the door. Priya carries a small canvas bag.

PRIYA
Are you ready, Aanya? Today is the day.

AANYA
Quiet Day at the Botanical Glasshouse. The glass keeps the city noise out. Only twenty people are allowed inside at one time. No talking.

PRIYA
(Smiling)
I have your map ready.

Aanya takes a hand-drawn map from her desk. It shows the quietest side-streets in the city. Every street is colored in soft blue.

AANYA
I want to find the quietest corner. I want to hear the silence. It feels like cool water.

EXT. CITY STREET - DAY

Aanya and Priya walk down the pavement. 

Aanya walks with a steady rhythm. One step. Two steps. Three steps. Four. She avoids the cracks with practiced ease.

She turns the smooth grey pebble in her pocket. It is cool and comforting.

They approach a busy intersection.

The pedestrian traffic light flashes. It does not flash normally.

Blink. Blink. Pause. Blink. Blink. Pause.

Aanya stops. She stares at the red hand on the signal.

PRIYA
Come on, sweetie. We have to cross before the timer ends.

AANYA
No. The timer is wrong. It is not counting down. It is playing a beat.

PRIYA
It is probably just a loose wire. The city has old systems.

AANYA
Loose wires make messy blinks. This is yellow, yellow, red, blink, yellow. It is a sequence. Two short, one long, one blank.

Across the street, another traffic signal begins to blink in the exact same pattern.

EXT. TOWN SQUARE - DAY

They reach the town square. It is a major intersection.

Every traffic light in sight is now blinking in unison.

Yellow, yellow, red, blink. Yellow, yellow, red, blink.

Cars stop abruptly. Drivers look confused. A delivery truck screeches to a halt.

Then, the honking begins. 

One car honks. Then ten. Then fifty.

The noise rises like a wave. It bounces off the concrete buildings.

Aanya pulls her noise-reducing headphones over her ears. She presses them tightly against her head. But the sound is too loud. It is a physical force.

For Aanya, the sensory overload is not a tantrum. It is a visual storm.

The car honks transform into giant, sharp spikes of grey light. They shoot up from the street, piercing the air.

The flashing yellow lights stretch into jagged, spinning triangles that fill her vision.

The world is breaking into loud, chaotic geometric shapes.

Aanya drops to her knees. She closes her eyes. She reaches into her pocket.

She squeezes the smooth grey pebble. She feels its flat, solid weight. She focuses on the cold surface of the stone.

In her mind, she visualizes the grey spikes softening. The jagged triangles slow down. They dissolve back into the street.

She opens her eyes. The noise is still loud, but she has her bearings.

She looks at the flashing lights again. She sees through the chaos.

AANYA
The pattern is getting faster. The pause was four seconds. Now it is two. It is a countdown.

Priya kneels next to her, shielding her from the crowd.

PRIYA
Let's go home, Aanya. We can try the Glasshouse next week.

AANYA
No. If we go home, the noise will follow us. The whole city will be loud. The Glasshouse will not be quiet. I have to fix the rhythm.

PRIYA
How can we fix it?

AANYA
(Pointing across the square)
The Transit Control Hub. That is where the pattern comes from.

INT. TRANSIT CONTROL HUB - DAY

Alarms beep in high-pitched sequences. Red warning lights flash on the walls.

MR. HENDERSON (50s), the chief transit engineer, paces back and forth in front of a giant wall of monitors.

MR. HENDERSON
The main server is glitching! The traffic signals are completely out of control! It is a total system failure!

Aanya and Priya enter the control room. 

MR. HENDERSON
Hey! Who let you in? This room is closed to the public!

PRIYA
Please, sir. My daughter can help. She understands patterns.

MR. HENDERSON
I do not have time for children. The entire city grid is about to lock up. I have to shut down the main power grid to reset it.

AANYA
If you shut down the power, the trains will stop. The sirens will start. The city will be too loud.

MR. HENDERSON
It is the only way to clear the glitch!

AANYA
It is not a glitch. The system is stuck in a loop. It is playing a Fibonacci sequence. One, one, two, three, five. 

Mr. Henderson stops. He looks at Aanya, then at his monitors.

MR. HENDERSON
What did you say?

AANYA
The next pause in the lights will be exactly eight seconds. Watch the screen for Sector Four.

Mr. Henderson looks at the monitor for Sector Four. 

A timer counts down. 

AANYA
Five. Four. Three. Two. One. Now.

The monitor flashes. The screen goes completely blank. A timer at the bottom freezes at exactly 8.00 seconds.

Mr. Henderson stares at the screen. His jaw drops.

MR. HENDERSON
How did you know that? Our diagnostic tools could not predict that.

AANYA
I listened to the beat. The city is trying to reset itself, but the code is trapped in a loop. It needs the inverse key to balance the rhythm.

MR. HENDERSON
The manual override console is locked. It requires a manual rhythmic input. We do not have the sequence.

AANYA
I have it. 

Aanya steps up to the primary control console. 

She looks at the grid of glowing buttons. She does not hesitate. She has the agency to fix this.

She taps her fingers on the metal casing first, finding the tempo.

Tap. Tap. Tap-tap. Tap-tap-tap.

She presses the flashing buttons on the console in a precise, rhythmic order.

She uses her fingers to type the inverse sequence. Eight. Five. Three. Two. One.

With each press, a soft, musical tone chime sounds from the console.

She hits the final blue button.

Click.

The red warning lights on the walls turn to a soft, steady green.

The alarms stop beeping.

On the main monitor wall, the traffic signals return to their normal, gentle cycle. Green. Yellow. Red.

Outside, the sound of honking fades into quiet engine hums.

Mr. Henderson looks at Aanya. He looks at her with immense respect.

MR. HENDERSON
You solved it. You read the system when none of our computers could.

AANYA
I like patterns. They always tell the truth.

PRIYA
(Proudly)
Thank you, Aanya.

AANYA
Now, we have thirty minutes left of Quiet Day.

INT. BOTANICAL GLASSHOUSE - DAY

Sunlight streams through the high glass dome. Huge, green ferns hang from the ceiling. A small, clear waterfall trickles into a stone basin.

It is completely, beautifully silent.

Aanya sits on a wooden bench in the center of the glasshouse.

She is autistic, peaceful, and entirely herself. Her jumper sleeves are still over her hands.

She holds the smooth grey pebble in her open palm, letting the cool glasshouse air wash over her.

She closes her eyes.

The silence around her is a beautiful, soft blue shape.